<section class="how">
  <h2>How to Use This D&D Name Generator</h2>
  <p>Select the race that matches your character. Not sure yet? Try generating a few batches of different races and see which name clicks. Good character names often choose you.</p>
  <p>The gender filter changes the phonetic style of the output without being strictly male or female in a real-world sense. Think of it as a guide for the naming convention rather than a hard rule. Many players mix conventions for non-binary or gender-fluid characters, and that works perfectly fine here.</p>
  <p>Generated names can be used directly or treated as inspiration. Taking a generated name and modifying one syllable often produces something that feels more personal and custom while still fitting the lore.</p>
</section>

<section class="formula">
  <h2>How D&D Race Names Are Structured</h2>
  <p><strong>Elf names</strong> use soft vowel-heavy syllables with flowing transitions. The Player's Handbook lists names like Adran, Aelar, Ara, Eres, Faral, Immeral, Ivellios, Laucian, Mindartis. The pattern tends toward two to three syllables with no harsh consonant clusters.</p>
  <p><strong>Dwarf names</strong> are short, hard, and often end in consonants. PHB examples include Barendd, Brottor, Morgran, Orsik. Many include double letters and guttural sounds. Family clan names are often passed down and treated as surnames.</p>
  <p><strong>Tiefling names</strong> follow two conventions. Classical names derived from Infernal sounds harsh and exotic. Virtue names chosen after birth are words like Carrion, Excellence, Hope, Penance, or Reverence. Both are valid.</p>
  <p><strong>Human names</strong> vary by region in the campaign world but generally follow real-world cultural patterns. The generator draws from a broad range of medieval European and Forgotten Realms regional styles.</p>
</section>

