<section class="faq">
  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <h3>Are these D&D names lore accurate?</h3>
  <p>The names are based on phonetic patterns from the official D&D 5e Player's Handbook and race-specific lore guides. Elven names use soft flowing syllables, dwarven names use hard consonants and monosyllabic roots, and tiefling names follow the virtue naming tradition. They are designed to sound authentic within D&D settings without directly copying copyrighted proper nouns.</p>

  <h3>Can I use these names for other fantasy games?</h3>
  <p>Absolutely. The names work well for Pathfinder, Baldur's Gate, Elder Scrolls, fantasy novels, and any tabletop or video game setting that uses high fantasy races. The race categories map naturally to most fantasy universes even if the names differ slightly from setting-specific conventions.</p>

  <h3>How many names can I generate at once?</h3>
  <p>You can generate up to 20 names per click. Click Generate again for a completely new batch. Each result is randomly assembled from curated syllable lists specific to each race, so repeats are rare even across many generations.</p>

  <h3>What is a tiefling name and how are they structured?</h3>
  <p>In D&D lore, tieflings often take names that reflect virtues or concepts, like Ambition, Despair, Excellence, or Glory. This is a naming tradition they adopt themselves, often as a statement about who they choose to be. The generator includes both virtue-style names and phonetically infernal-sounding names with harder consonants.</p>

  <h3>What is the difference between male and female elf names in D&D?</h3>
  <p>Female elven names typically end in softer sounds and vowels. Male elven names often use stronger endings. The generator respects these conventions when you select a gender filter, though many players blend these conventions freely, especially for non-binary characters.</p>
</section>

